build/
src/build/
src/build-arm64/
# the development root a CUDA build on Windows drives with ninja: a generated
# tree like every other build root, and its name is long on the same grounds.
src/build-cuda/
# the cpu-only shape's root, named by run.bat's nodevice word: generated like
# every other build root.
src/build-nd/
# the development path's card-named roots: run.bat and build-rel.bat now name
# the same root for one shape, so a root like build-c75 is generated state
# exactly like every other build root and is never a source. these stand at
# the repository top since the folder move; the src/ spellings stay for a tree
# that has not moved its roots yet.
build-c*/
src/build-c*/
# the release path's plain-vulkan cpu root on windows, same generated state.
build-p/
src/build-p/
# the release path's own source-tree build root: kept shallow on the same
# grounds as the development one, and never a source.
src/.build/
# the release roots named for one target each: the plain cpu flavour and one
# folder per card generation, generated like every other build root.
src/.build-p/
src/.build-c*/
# the harness's own scratch root, shallow like the rest of the dot roots: a
# fresh configure here is the only honest reading of PASS/FAIL.
src/.build-git-tests/
Release/.build/
*.exe
# the object library the visual studio builder lays under a bin folder is
# generated state, not an artifact of the release path: the release ships
# archives, which stand under Release/ unpatterned and committed.
bin/**/*.obj
# an .obj left beside its source by a hand-run cl.exe is generated state like
# every other built byte, wherever it lands.
*.obj
src/open-local-services
src/open-local-services.new
# the development tree runs the way the release folder will: with the image
# engine copied beside the source it links against. built bytes, laid down by
# run.bat on every build, never a source - under the split these wear the
# engine's shape name, stable-diffusion-cuda.dll and its siblings on windows,
# libstable-diffusion-cuda.so and its twins on linux.
src/stable-diffusion*.dll
src/libstable-diffusion*.so*
src/open-local-services.exe
weights/
data/
/vendor/
!webui/vendor/
.env

# the release artifacts are built bytes, not source: build-rel.sh and
# build-rel.bat lay them under Release/ on the box that built them - a server
# binary and the engines beside it run to hundreds of megabytes each. the
# finished ARCHIVES are the release and are committed - the repository is the
# release - with these bounds, each one read off a measured fact:
#   - the loose target folders stay out: an archive and its own unzipped
#     folder are the same bytes twice, and the archive is the release.
#   - cuda archives stay out wherever the tree clones: cuBLAS, cuBLASLt and
#     cuFFT measure some 800 MB compressed per CUDA shape, and GitHub refuses
#     any single file past 100 MB outright. they live beside the tree, carry
#     their sha256 in the committed SHA256SUMS-<os>.txt manifest, and reach a
#     machine over the link - a clone can hold the source of every artifact,
#     not the heaviest artifacts themselves.
#   - the vulkan and cpu archives (~50 MB) fit both bounds and are committed.
# build-info.txt beside them names the commit everything was made from.
Release/*/
Release/*cuda*.zip
Release/*cuda*.tar.gz

# bin/ is the staging room for /api/update: run.bat and build-rel.* lay a
# fresh build there, and the hand moves it into place while the server is
# down. built bytes never ride the clone - the source builds them where they
# stand.
bin/

# the owner's reference manuals: 110 MB of PDFs that reach a machine by copy
# over the link like the other payload trees, never by clone. what the
# maintainer tab reads is held under data/maint once a manual is brought in,
# so a clone on another box has no use for these bytes.
ref/

# the working notes an agent keeps for itself beside the tree: behaviour rules,
# the map of this environment. Not part of the product, and written for the
# machine that is doing the work, so it is never carried in a clone.
local.md

# what the picture suite wrote the last time it ran: one line per run, laid
# beside the script that made it, so a person reads what the machine answered.
# Machine state like every other log, and never part of a clone.
*.log

# the second scratch ground the test harness falls back to when the machine's
# temp refuses it: built-state, disposable, never part of a clone.
ols-test-scratch/
